/*
:name: flop_sim_peek_secondary
:type: simulation
*/

// todo/livehd/2f-latch — SILENT SIM BUG TRACKER (2026-07-25 audit).
// FIXED 2026-08-02; the failure it tracked is now STRUCTURALLY IMPOSSIBLE
// (peek() no longer exists). Kept as a live multi-clock regression.
//
// READING A DUT OUTPUT BEFORE `step` ATE A SECOND-DOMAIN CLOCK EDGE.
//
// WHAT WAS WRONG. Every output read in a test block used to become its own
// `peek()` call, and `peek()` ran a full `cycle()` and then rolled the state
// back. It snapshotted flops, pipeline stages, memories and the VCD tick — but
// NOT the per-net previous-clock-level bit (`__clkprev_<net>`) that M6's
// second-domain edge detection depends on. So the peek's `cycle()` advanced that
// bit, the rollback left it advanced, and the real `step()` that followed saw no
// edge. The second-domain flop then never committed, silently. The same gap hit
// checkpointing: the bit is in neither `dump_state` nor `load_state`, so a
// `--restart-cycle` / `--vcd-from` restore gained or lost commits.
//
// It was nastier than the other trackers because the DESIGN is fine — it was
// triggered purely by an innocuous testbench habit (looking at an output before
// stepping), which is exactly what a human debugging a waveform does.
//
// WHY IT CANNOT RECUR. An output read is now a reference into the `__out`
// member that `cycle()` settles on its way out. A read runs NO design code at
// all, so it cannot advance an edge-detection bit — there is no state-restoring
// recompute left to get wrong. The asserts below still pin the multi-clock
// commit behavior, and the pre-step read is retained deliberately so the shape
// that used to break stays covered.
//
// CONTROL: `qa` is on the reference clock and is unaffected, so a failure below
// is specifically the second-domain previous-level bit.
//
// The companion fixture inou/prp/tests/sim/multiclock_two_domain.prp is the
// SAME design shape WITHOUT a pre-step read, and it passes — that pair is the
// proof that the read is what breaks it.
pub mod pksec(clkb:bool, d:u8) -> (qa:u8@[1], qb:u8@[2]) {
  reg a:u8 = 0
  reg b:u8:[clock_pin=ref clkb] = 0
  qa = a
  qb = b
  a  = d
  b  = a
}

test pksec.pre_step_read_must_not_consume_a_clock_edge {
  mut acc = pksec
  mut pre = 0
  tick 4 {
    acc.d    = 11 + (17 * clock)
    acc.clkb = (clock == 2)          // one rise, at tick 2
    // THE TRIGGER: an output read BEFORE `step`. Each such read is a peek(),
    // and peek() advances the second domain's previous-level bit without
    // restoring it. The read must be UNCONDITIONAL -- an `implies` guard
    // lowers to `||`, which short-circuits, so a guarded read only peeks on the
    // ticks where the guard is true and the bug hides.
    pre = acc.qb
    step
    // CONTROL: the reference-clock stage is unaffected by the peek.
    assert(acc.qa == 11 + (17 * clock), "the reference-clock stage still commits every tick (control)")
    // THE BUG: clkb rose at tick 2, so the second-domain flop must have
    // committed by tick 3. Today the pre-step peek consumed that edge.
    assert((clock >= 3) implies (acc.qb != 0), "a pre-step read must not consume the second domain's clock edge")
  }
  // At the final iteration this is the value captured on clkb's tick-2 rise.
  // Pin the value (rather than the pre-fix zero) so the trigger remains an
  // observable read without encoding the bug into the control assertion.
  assert(pre == 28, "the pre-step read observes the already-committed second-domain value")
}
